Chapter: WarmupLamTvar

 The smallest program that makes the zig plug name a type variable in a
 scope where nothing declares it.

 A comprehension desugars to `map-list f xs`. When `f` is a NAMED
 definition, the plug recovers map-list's own `a` from that function's
 declared parameter type and emits a concrete type argument. When `f` is
 a LAMBDA it is lifted to a `__lam_N` definition by the DRIVER, and the
 lift runs after resolve, so the lifted definition's parameter carries
 the type it was HANDED -- map-list's own `a`, still a TypeVar -- rather
 than a resolved one. CSharpEmitter.codex says this above `is-lam-def`
 and answers `dynamic`; zig has no `dynamic`.

 The lift is why this file has to reach the plug through the seed. The
 IR pipeline does not lift, so `native/codexir` emits the lambda inline
 and this program transpiles clean there.

 `mapped-named` is the control and `mapped-lambda` the defect row: same
 map-list, same element type, and the only difference is whether the
 mapped function has a name.

 The control calls map-list directly. A comprehension does NOT make one:
 `for x in xs -> bump x` builds a lambda around the call rather than
 handing `bump` over, so it lifts like any other and both rows would be
 defect rows -- which is what the first version of this file measured.

Section: Main

  bump : Integer -> Integer
  bump (n) = n + 1

  mapped-named : List Integer -> List Integer
  mapped-named (xs) = map-list bump xs

  mapped-lambda : List Integer -> List Integer
  mapped-lambda (xs) = for x in xs -> x + 1

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line-uni (show (list-at (mapped-named [1, 2, 3]) 2))
   print-line-uni (show (list-at (mapped-lambda [1, 2, 3]) 2))
  end
